ence data and I use GBS as an  example of that  I put up GPS data and I showed okay we  can use GPS for genomic selection in  guiles breeding program and we can also  use that same sequence deep data with  the practical haplotype graph in Geils  breeding program so this year I'm not  going to show you GPS data with the  practical haplotype graph but I am going  to use that GPS data as our baseline we  know that gb/s is a really an effective  technology and so we're always trying to  get prediction accuracies with new  technologies that are at least  equivalent to that okay so I'll start  actually by talking about skin sequence  and when I talk about skin sequence what  I'm really talking about is a protocol  that was designed by NextEra and  essentially they take a TN v transpose  ace that jumps into the genome at  different positions randomly across the  entire genome and then we sequence out  from those positions and so that gives  us a very sparse initial data set and  it's also completely random so we're not  capturing one specific or consistent  marker set with this technology but it  is really cheap it cost less than $10  per sample and so it is a viable option  or something that we'd like to be able  to use with with a breeding program and  so the question at the bottom then  becomes does imputation with the  practical haplotype graph make it  possible to use this technology so I'm  gonna put up this slide first this is a  plot very similar to one I showed you  last year and both in this plot and in  plots for the rest of my talk if the if  the box is blue its GBS data so that's  just the baseline okay GBS works well  for prediction accuracy in Gaia's  program now if I add in boxes for that  next Arace skim sequence data that's  those green boxes on the bottom and you  can see that yes this technology is less  than $10 per sample but it's not giving  us anything it's completely useless for  prediction okay and that really comes  down to the fact that because 