ght now the  practical applicable Andamanese and so  this is something that actually the  programmers have been have changed  actually how we're imputing these  genotypes and so in the next couple  months I'll be looking at if those  changes have actually improved this kind  of plot but the answer right now is that  no with ramp seek the practical  haplotype graph doesn't really help us  and isn't really necessary at all so  that brings us to this nice point where  we have  a couple different options for  genotyping and the option that a breeder  chooses really should be designed or  should be chosen based on both their  breeding goals and the budget that  they're willing to spend so I'm going to  build this plot a little bit up on the  right here on the x-axis we have the  number of snips it only goes to 3,000  but you can imagine we could extend that  fairly significantly and then on the  y-axis we have costs per sample okay so  I'll be plotting data that comes from a  group Lex genomics that's one of the  companies that produces this are a champ  seek amplicon based sequencing and as I  plot those I've also colored hopefully  you can see it okay the background of  this plot to show you at that location  in the graph what is our best sequencing  option cost wise okay and so just as an  example if we had a point where this X  is on the screen that means we want more  than 2,000 markers and we're willing to  pay more than $15 per sample and so GBS  is a good genotyping strategy in that  particular case does that make sense  great so let's add some data if we start  adding data we can see that if we're  working in a species that has a lot of  breeding efforts going on maybe we  expect that across all breeding programs  there could be 80,000 or even a hundred  thousand individuals that are being  genotyped then we're following the blue  and purple lines on this plot and  actually are a champ seek is going to be  our best bet we can get about 2,000  markers from our a 